This study introduces an innovative analytical delay model specifically tailored for deep submicron CMOS inverters. Developed based on the critical recognition that inverter delay is a complex interplay of multiple process variables and load capacitance, the proposed model incorporates parameterized equations derived using Curve Fitting Toolbox in Matlab to accurately capture these dynamics. In comparison to simulation results generated by SPICE and the BSIM4 transistor model, the analytical delay model exhibits remarkably high precision, demonstrating an average error of less than 2% across an extensive range of technological parameters and output loads. As a result, the proposed model presents itself as a computationally efficient solution for diverse technology nodes and probabilistic gate delay characterisation applications. Furthermore, the analytical delay model can be seamlessly integrated with probabilistic static power estimation techniques to predict the stochastic behaviour of integrated circuits due to variability in static power consumption. Moreover, the proposed model offers potential applications in the fast and efficient evaluation of inverter performance across an array of transistor widths within integrated circuits, a phenomenon that has been empirically identified but lacks theoretical justification. This research aims to bridge the present gap in the inverter delay domain by deriving the first-ever analytical delay model that correlates inverter delay with transistor width, a phenomenon that has not been studied before, to the best of the researchers' knowledge. The derived width-dependent delay model can be used in statistical power estimation, but a comprehensive discussion on this topic is presented in Section 6.3.3. This research also aims to contribute to the ongoing research on the impact of variability on inverter delay, particularly on deep submicron CMOS inverters, an area that has been relatively less studied in the relevant literature. The proposed model can be downloaded from: \\nopsongang.github.io/DelayModel download. A similar effort has also led to the development of an analytical area model through a different approach and is made available at: \\nopsongang.github.io/AreaModel download. The remaining sections of this paper are organized to first present the background and motivation for this research, followed by the proposed analytical delay model. The model is then validated against SPICE simulations and BSIM4 transistor model. A related but independent topic is presented in Section 6.3.3, specifically the efficient characterization of inverter performance using an analytical delay model. The conclusions of this research are presented in Section 7. 

