We introduce an upgraded RingCT protocol that supports stealth addresses in conjunction with Delegated Proof of Stake, resulting in Delegated RingCT.
The Delegated RingCT scheme is comprised of an integrated signature and encryption framework, combining a public key encryption protocol and two types of signatures along with linkable ring signatures, alongside non-interactive zero-knowledge proofs.
A comprehensive description of Delegated RingCT, along with security evidence and an implemented prototype, are presented, which we subsequently test through benchmarking analysis.
While Delegated RingCT may not possess the same level of anonymity compared to other RingCT implementations, we propose that its ability to be compatible with DPoS consensus mechanisms provides a reasonable trade-off for enabling a more efficient and scalable decentralised cryptocurrency with preserved anonymity capabilities. As such, Delegated RingCT might suit a future scalable cryptocurrency.} 
Note: The Delegated RingCT protocol has been successfully deployed on BitShares in October 2019. Further information regarding its implementation, including performance results, is available at https://github.com/blockchainzombie/DelegatedRingCT/wiki. Additionally, this work has been presented at the 13th Symposium on Foundations of Computer Security, held on September 6, 2019.}

We propose a supersymmetric expansion of a basic $U(1)_X$ model with a new, $Z_2$ symmetry, where both the $U(1)_X$ gauge symmetry and the R-parity are spontaneously broken at the TeV scale through a right-handed neutrino with a $Z_2$ symmetry that's even. This results in the lightest right-handed neutrino acting as dark matter, while meeting all current experimental constraints. Additionally, we've found that the dark matter sector mirrors the lightest supersymmetric particle sector of a minimal R-symmetric model. Lastly, we've determined the parameter space allowing dark matter to consist of stau and right-handed neutrino, with the latter having a mass of approximately 500 GeV. This setup may potentially be tested by the Snowmass collider project. 

Note: This research has implications for understanding the origin of neutrino masses and the decay of dark matter, particularly if it's composed of right-handed neutrinos with masses less than 50 GeV. In such cases, it may be able to decay into both the Higgs boson and the $Z$ boson. If its mass lies in the range of 10-50 GeV, the upper bound on the flavor-changing neutral current is "soft", which means this setup has the potential for resolving the flavor puzzle. Further studies are required to explore the interesting features of this model, particularly its flavor physics. 

This setup has the following key features:
$\bullet$ Implies CoGeNeT signals, with the accidental $Z_2$ symmetry ensuring the stability of dark matter;
$\bullet$ Results in the disappearance of the neutrino less massive than the cold dark matter, with the accidental $Z_2$ symmetry ensuring its suppression;
$\bullet$ Provides a simple explanation for the anomalous magnetic moment of the neutrino;
$\bullet$ Results in the potential for the decay of the dark matter into the Higgs boson and the $Z$ boson;
$\bullet$ Provides a solution to the gravitational scattering problem. 

This setup has the following technical features:
$\bullet$ Uses the pseudo Nambu-Goldstone Boson (NGB) mechanism to break $U(1)_X$;
$\bullet$ Applies the Radiative Neutrino Mass mechanism to generate the neutrino mass;
$\bullet$ Uses the loop-level suppression to generate the neutrino mass;
$\bullet$ Provides a model to generate the neutrino mass; 
