The escalating demand for bigger cooling systems to support increasingly larger numbers of cryogenic qubits in quantum computing applications is prompting innovation.
Through the National Quantum Initiative and Department of Energy's support, our work at the new quantum research facility aims to build a 20-foot diameter, 30-foot high cryogenic storage platform that uses a cryogenic fluid flow facility to test heat transfer and fluid dynamics at very low temperatures.
This paper outlines the quantum computing cooling needs, the progress so far in designing and simulating the cryogenic storage platform, and the benefits of the cryogenic fluid flow facility in testing cryogenic flow phenomena, which will be tested at temperatures ranging from 1.5 to 50 Kelvin. The platform and facility will facilitate a range of experiments, including heat transfer and fluid dynamics analysis, at very low temperatures. Firstly, the quantum computing cooling requirements will be described. This will be followed by an update on the progress so far in designing and simulating the cryogenic storage platform, including the progress made in drafting the facility design, selecting the cooling systems, and performing numerical simulations of the platform's performance. This will be followed by an overview of the cryogenic fluid flow facility and the tests planned to conduct in this facility. The final section will discuss the platform and facility as a tool for education and training in cryogenic flow phenomena. The proposed platform and facility are an innovative opportunity to conduct cryogenic flow research that is currently missing from the field, and the new facility will also provide an exceptional teaching and training tool for the next generation of engineers that will be working with cryogenic flow phenomena in their future careers. The views, findings, and recommendations stated in this paper do not necessarily represent the views of the Department of Energy. 

\keywords{quantum computing \and cryogenic fluid flow \and cryogenic storage platform \and cryogenic cooling}
